    I strongly, deeply, fervently hope that you're not serious.  I think it's
    one thing to make an informed decision to experiment on oneself; I wouldn't
    for an instant consider experimenting on a child who is incapable of giving
    an informed consent.
    
    I'm not kidding here.  Do you have the slightest concept of how variable
    drug effects can be between adults and children?  Do you have any idea of
    how much variation there is between dosage levels for adults and children?
    
    If you _are_ considering this, I wish you'd stop.
    

I posed a series of questions to the list on parenting not because of
anything I'm doing or planning on doing or because I want your advice.
I asked because I was curious about what other people did, and will
shortly post on what I do.  However:

1.  An essential part of parenting is making decisions on behalf of your 
    children.  Every day you make decisions that will affect your children's
    health, happiness, and continued existence.  A decision to give your
    children nootropics is no different than a decision to give your children
    large quantities of sugar, to tell your kids about AIDS, to buy a car
    with passenger-side airbags, or as to what school they go.

    You ARE experimenting with their lives, and they do not give informed
    consent.

2.  Not to decide is to decide.  By not giving your kids nootropics, you are
    making a decision concerning their lives that is as profound as deciding
    to give them nootropics.

3.  The term nootropics encompasses vitamins and nutrients as well as commercial
    pharmaceuticals.  For example, lecithin, ginseng, B complex, and caffeine are 
    nootropics.  If you let your kids drink Coke, you are giving them nootropics.
    Many nootropics naturally occur in food.
    

