>>>Superficially, as measured in terms of standardized test scores, the
schools in several European and Asian nations seem to produce
better-educated graduates,<<<

>>>Standardized tests are not everything.<<<

The actual tests European students take are less standardized than ours.  
They use essay questions rather than the multiple guess tests we have to
use because our students (and test markers) can't read or write.  Essays
are a better measure of creativity than are multiple choice exams.

>>>Americans lag the rest of the First World (Europe, Japan) based on 
test scores at the elementary and secondary level. By the end of college,
Americans have generally caught up with their counterparts in Europe and
Japan, for various reasons (mentioned below). By the end of grad school,
American-educated students are way ahead (by many measures, and 
reflected in the influx of foreign students to American grad programs).<<<

US college students *begin* their elementary & secondary education at the
college level.  Bonehead English, etc.  Only a small number go to graduate
school (and half our graduate students in math and science are foreigners).
The schools may be OK at that level, the students aren't.  

In the UK, if you enter the Royal College of Physicians & Surgeons, you are
in *medical school* at 18 - not 4 years later as in the US.  

What about the vast majority who don't go to grad school (or go to grad 
school in the "soft" subjects) and can't comfortably read and write.

I make my living supplying declarative sentences to engineering graduates 
who are unable to produce them for themselves.

>>>in other First World countries, less academically skilled students are 
routed early on into vocational training, which tends to skew many of the 
reports about how well-educated they are compared to American high school 
students).<<<

Unfortunately, tests of 12-year-olds show the same gap.  In many cases,
the *average* Japanese student does better than the 95th percentile US
student.

>>>creativity--the ability to synthesize new ideas from learned material
--becomes more important as educational level rises...any attempt to make 
our primary and secondary schools more Japanese- or European-like, by 
emphasizing rote learning over creativity and inquisitiveness, is a move in 
the _wrong_ direction. <<<

If you know nothing, you have nothing to be creative with.  In order to
read, one must memorize the sounds of the phonemes (c-a-t spells cat).
Knowing how to calculate is the first step to playing with math.  Americans
were just as creative before 1930 when the introduction of the Look-Say
method of teaching reading raised the illiteracy rate (as measured by
the Armed Forces Qualification Tests) from 0.4% (1941 troopies) to 17%
(1951 troopies).  This is a 425% increase in 10 years.

Being well-read didn't hurt Shakespeare's creativity or Newton's science.

What percentage of major US orchestra conductors are foreign born (+90%).
What percentage of 'US' Nobel Prize winners are foreign educated (+50%).

Funny you should mention quadratic equations.  Heinlein once suggested
that voters be required to solve a randomly generated quadratic equation 
before they could vote.  He said that anyone who couldn't or wouldn't
learn how to solve one would not be able to render good judgments in
other areas.

Most US schools below the university level are uninterested in teaching 
*anything*.  There will be many different approaches once we have competitive
schools.  That is their purpose to offer choice.

I would keep my child home rather than waste the time driving him to a 
"creative" school.  He could be creative at home for less time and money.
All schools should be commercial and we should go to them (or log on to
them) when we want specific things taught - reading, Latin, or quadratic 
equations.


