Date: Wed, 17 Feb 93 11:36:48 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: IP/VR: Trade dress

About a week ago I posted a message in which, following up on Tim May's
lead, I speculated on the impacts that advancing technology would have on
intellectual property.  I brushed aside trademark law, saying I did not
expect it to change very much.  It is not an area of IP law one normally
associates with high tech; it usually concerns mundane things like the
colors of soup can labels.  But I recently read something in WIRED that
suggests otherwise.

The WIRED article noted that a recent supreme court decision with respect
to trade dress (an area of trademark law) could give rise to enforceable
rights to the "look and feel" of VR environments.  In TWO PESOS V TACO
CABANA, INC., the Supreme Court held that a restaurant could hold a
trademark in the nonfunctional, distinctive elements that together made up
its "atmosphere."  Here those elements included "a festive eating
atmosphere having interior dining and patio areas decorated with artifacts,
bright colors, paintings and murals.  The patio includes interior and
exterior areas with the interior patio capable of being sealed off from the
outside patio by overhead garage doors.  The stepped exterior of the
building is a festive and vivid color scheme using top border paint and
neon stripes.  Bright awnings and umbrellas continue the theme."  A student
from the area where these two restaurants had been in competition confirmed
that they were virtually (if you'll pardon the pun) indistinguishable.  The
Supreme Court gave Taco Cabana the right to prevent Two Pesos from
borrowing its trade dress and free riding off of the image it had carefully
cultivated.

The writer in WIRED noted that the same ruling should apply with equal
force in VR environments.  If you come up with a particular "atmosphere"
for your VR, TWO PESOS seems indicates that you could get trademark
protection for it.  Note that this trade dress cannot serve a functional
purpose--you cannot get trademark protection for actually useful
attributes.

[I have uncharacteristically left out cites in this post because I left my
copy of WIRED in SF with my SO.  I assumed that I would be able to pull it
up on-line when I returned to Chicago, but (*gasp*!) WIRED is not on NEXIS!
 If anyone *really* has to have a cite, I'll contact my SO and get it.]


