Date: Wed, 17 Feb 93 17:13:59 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ENTREPRENEURIAL: Polar Springs and Financing

>When not drinking large quantities of plain water (try Poland Springs:
>it tastes much better than most, and is reasonably pure and inexpensive),

This reminds me of a company called "Polar Spring," which is trying to get
financing for a water purification appliance, dubbed "Crystallix.". That in
and of itself barely rates mention, except for the similarity with Poland
Springs, but their experiences relate to some themes on this list.

I saw their pitch at a "New Enterprise Forum" at Stanford B-School,
sponsored by the M.I.T. Club of Northern California (no, I'm not an M.I.T.
grad). (This is a place where would-be entrepreneurs get to meet investors,
venture capitalists, and interested engineers and managers.)

Some points:

* water purification in kitchen appliance form factor--like a small bread
machine or Cuisinart. No bottles, no filters to clean or dispose of.

* uses _freezing_ to produce pure water. (Similar desalinization plants
exist, based on continuous processing...the Polar Spring idea is a simple,
and patented, scheme for batch processing, e.g., a quart or so at a time.)
This method, very similar to "zone refining," is 7 times more efficient
than distillation.

* cost for a counter-top system: about $400 (which may be why it hasn't
gotten off the ground).

* they've approached Arrowhead (a division of Perrier), Alhambra, etc. All
have expressed "interest," but no funding. The pair who started Polar
Spring have raised $500K in a "family and friends" placement with 35
friends and small investors. Still, they figure they need much more to
launch the product.

* they may seek stop-gap funding by pursuing a NASA contract for the Space
Station (which needs water recycling and is interested in the ice method
used by Polar Spring.)

(P.S. to "Oceania" ocean-going colonizers: You may be thinking this
approach could be use of ships at sea. It could, but ships usually have
boilers for propulsion and hence lots of surplus heat, so distillation
systems have a real edge.)

Most of us in the room (about 75 investors, entrepreneurs, engineers, etc.)
were very impressed, and puzzled that this has not taken off. The
experiences of the founders were highly revealing.

Getting a start-up going is never easy, and apparently even with a
well-defined product, a known market, and several fully-operational
prototypes, they haven't been able to get funding. Sobering.

I mention this not to discourage you entrepreneurs out there, but just as
general information which may help in some way.

-Tim


