From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: POLI: Customs / As bad as oppressive police?

   From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)

   Dave Worley responds:
   >I would rephrase it as "With customs that I have to obey, why would I
   >need a government to oppress me?"  If there is no practical way to
   >avoid obedience, it's just as good as the police.

   Um.  Obedience?  Oppression?  Customary law does not entail obedience
   or oppression.  Customary law is a "language of interaction" that evolves
   to facilitate mutally-beneficial interaction between people and to reduce
   violent conflict.  What people choose to do with their own lives is commonly
   not a matter of concern for such systems.  Also, if customs really were
   oppressive then this would create the incentives to develop new customs to
   resolve the problem.

   Tell you what.  If you were to read the _Enterprise of Law_ by
   Bruce Benson, then we could have a great discussion on this topic.  If
   you already have, then please tell us why you think that customary law
   is (or can be) oppressive, and what you mean by oppression.

   I'll leave everyone with an exerpt from the book.

That's all cool, but it's pretty theoretical.  I haven't investigated
it in detail, but it seems that the systems of "customary law" that
I'm familiar tend to be quite constrictive.  The top example is the
Icelandic Allthing (altho that's not its right name); it is presented
as a good example of customary law, but it outright legislated what
religion everybody should have.  If that's not oppressive, I don't
know what is.

Other examples that come to mind are: Japanese social customs.  The
ancient Hebrew legal system.  The War on Drugs (my impression is that
if the majority had its way, drug dealing would be punished by
immediate execution).  The War on Pornography.  In all these cases,
the bulk of the population has decided that it is important to
regulate something that I consider a "freedom"; that's what I call
"oppressive".

Maybe I'm wrong.  But if a sizable majority gets a bug up its butt
about something (no matter how silly), it could easily turn into
customs enforced by the death penalty or something similarly noxious.
I think that the theoretical analysis forgets certain factors that may
be very important in the "real world".  "Widespread acceptance" does
come from perceived benefits, but that perceived benefit may be
irrational or oppressive; it doesn't necessarily come from a
calculation of economic benefit.  For instance, "not having people of
a different ethnicity living next door" has been perceived as a
benefit by large fractions of the world's populace, and they have
frequently been willing to enforce the custom of segregation by
killing violators.

A grimmer example is to think back on the history of "restrictive
covenants" in real estate.  These are often touted as a libertarian
alternative to zoning.  (Which is quite correct.)  But one has to
remember that a common purpose of restrictive covenants was to prevent
Jews or blacks from moving into a area, as well.

